# .surface-breaking — the changelog of surface we have deliberately TAKEN AWAY.
#
# scripts/check-surface.sh fails on any fact that disappears from the surface,
# because someone out there has it in a script. To remove one on purpose, paste
# the exact `.surface` line here under a dated comment saying why, then run
# `scripts/check-surface.sh --update`.
#
# Two rules keep this file honest:
#   * an entry only counts while the line is genuinely gone. Listing a line that
#     still exists is an error (a stale acknowledgement would pre-authorise a
#     future removal nobody reviewed).
#   * entries are never deleted after the fact — this file is the record of what
#     a user's old script may be relying on.
#
# Format: one verbatim .surface line per row.
#
# 2026-07-28 — nothing removed yet.

# 2026-07-28 — two notes REPLACED because they were false, not because the
# surface went away. Both are restated in the same column, corrected:
#
#   * the %global note claimed `--agent` wraps the response and `--json` never
#     changes shape. A `--batch` run emits bare JSONL under `--agent` and, on
#     failure, exits 3 with no {"ok":false} at all; and `--fields` projects and
#     re-serialises `.data` through jq. The skill already said the batch part
#     correctly, so the CLI's own docs contradicted each other.
#   * the tasks note said "neither resolution happens ... for an argument that
#     already looks like an id". That is now true only of a READ: a WRITE
#     probes an id-shaped argument for an exact name match and refuses a
#     collision (--by-id / --by-name decide).
note %global `--agent` wraps the response in {ok,data,meta,summary,breadcrumbs}; `--json` stays the RAW API body and never changes
note tasks neither resolution happens on a write, in a `--batch`, or for an argument that already looks like an id

# 2026-08-03 — BEHAVIOUR REMOVED, no fact removed. Recorded here anyway.
#
# No `.surface` line changed, so check-surface.sh is silent about this and
# always would be: the surface table describes commands and flags, and what
# changed is what the CLI DOES when nobody asked it to do anything. That is a
# blind spot in the ratchet, not an absence of breakage — hence this note.
#
# uku no longer installs updates by itself (C1).
#
#   was:  once a day, on almost any command, a newer VERSION triggered
#         cmd_update → `curl -fsSL <url> | sh`, unattended.
#   now:  the same daily check prints one line naming the new version and
#         `uku update`, and installs nothing.
#
# UKU_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 keeps its meaning — it now silences the notice and skips
# the check, so anyone who already set it sees no change at all.
#
# Who this can break: a machine that relied on picking up releases with no
# human in the loop (CI images, agent sandboxes) now stays on its installed
# version until something runs `uku update`. That is the intended cost. The
# unattended path fetched a remote script and piped it into `sh` on a machine
# holding a live API key, with every integrity control living inside the
# artefact being fetched — which cannot protect the choice of artefact.
#
# Also changed, same release, same reason:
#   * UKU_INSTALL_URL_DEFAULT no longer resolves through getuku.com; it is
#     pinned to an immutable tag on the repo. A third-party deploy is no longer
#     in the path of code this CLI executes.
#   * UKU_UPDATE_URL_DEFAULT deliberately still tracks main. It is read and
#     compared, never executed, and pinning it would end update detection.
#
# Pinned by tests/cases/update-trust.sh §0, §6 and §7.
